# Publishing

While you build a site, a [page](/docs/ui/pages) embeds the CSS it needs in
its own `<head>`. That needs no setup, but the same few kilobytes travel
with every page and the browser cannot cache them. Before putting a site in
front of visitors, move the CSS into one file that is downloaded once.

There are two ways to get that file: a route that makes it on the fly, for
development, and a file written ahead of time, for publishing.

## A stylesheet route for development

`ui.stylesheet` is a controller that serves the CSS at a path of your
choosing:

```gleam
import howdy
import howdy/ui
import howdy/ui/theme

howdy.new()
|> howdy.controller(pages())
|> howdy.controller(
  ui.stylesheet(at: "/assets/ui.css", themes: theme.default_themes()),
)
```

Link it from your pages, and they stop embedding their styles:

```gleam
page.new("Orders")
|> page.stylesheet(at: "/assets/ui.css")
```

The route sends the theme variables, the base styles, and the CSS for every
class that has been used since the server started. It is sent with an
`etag` and `cache-control: no-cache`, so the browser checks on every page
load, gets a quick `304` when nothing has changed, and sees new styles as
soon as they exist.

Do not publish with this route. It only knows the classes that have been
rendered so far, on that one server. After a restart the file is nearly
empty until pages have been visited, and two servers behind a load balancer
can give different answers. Its use is to let you develop with a linked
stylesheet, so that what you build matches what you will publish.

## Writing the file

For publishing, `howdy/ui/export` writes the CSS to a file from a known
list of classes. Put a small script in your project:

```gleam
// src/tasks/css.gleam
import gleam/io
import howdy/ui/export
import howdy/ui/theme
import my_app/ui/all

pub fn main() -> Nil {
  let assert Ok(Nil) =
    export.new(theme.default_themes())
    |> export.classes(all.classes())
    |> export.write(to: "priv/public/assets/ui.css")
  io.println("wrote priv/public/assets/ui.css")
}
```

```sh
gleam run -m tasks/css
```

`export.new` takes the [themes](/docs/ui/themes) your pages offer, and
always includes the built-in [components](/docs/ui/components).
`export.classes` adds your own. The `all.classes()` list is generated for
you, as described in [Custom components](/docs/ui/custom-components). If
you have no components of your own, leave that line out.

Pass the same themes your pages use. The file holds the theme variables, so
a page offering a theme that is not in the file would have no colours.

The output is minified, and deterministic: the same classes give the same
bytes on any machine, whatever pages were rendered beforehand. That makes
it safe to commit to your repository, and to check in CI that it is up to
date. Run the script whenever you change a theme or a component.

Classes appear in the file in the order given: the built-in ones first,
then yours in the order of the list. When two rules are equally specific,
the later one wins, so the order matters if your classes override built-in
ones on the same element.

## Serving the file

Serve it like any other asset, with
`howdy/static`, and give it a long cache lifetime:

```gleam
import howdy/static

howdy.controller(
  static.new(from: "priv/public")
  |> static.max_age(seconds: 31_536_000)
  |> static.build,
)
```

A long lifetime means browsers will not notice when the file changes, so
change its URL whenever it does. A version in the query string is enough:

```gleam
page.new("Orders")
|> page.stylesheet(at: "/assets/ui.css?v=" <> my_app.version)
```

`page.stylesheet` uses the path exactly as given, so any cache-busting
scheme you already use for other assets works here too.

## Live components

[Live components](/docs/ui/live-components) carry their own CSS, for
exactly the classes their view uses. They are styled the same whichever of
the three approaches a page takes, and need nothing from the exported file.

{since="2.2"}
## Islands

[Islands](/docs/ui/islands) use the same class names in the browser as on
the server, so the file styles what the server renders. A class an island
only uses after it starts in the browser has its CSS added to the page by
the island, so islands need nothing more from the file either. Their
browser code is published separately: export the app with
`gleam run -m howdy/ui export`, which bundles the islands first.

## A checklist

- Every component module has a `classes` function that lists all its
  classes, including every variant.
- `all.gleam` is up to date. Run `gleam run -m howdy/ui add <name>` again,
  for a component you have already copied, after adding a module of your
  own.
- The export uses the same themes as your pages.
- `gleam run -m tasks/css` has been run since the last change, and the
  result is committed.
- Pages link the file with `page.stylesheet`, with a version in the URL.
- An app with islands is exported with `gleam run -m howdy/ui export`.

The sign of a missed step is a component that looks right in development
and unstyled once published. Its class is missing from the file, usually
because it is not in a `classes` list.
